title ALES131 — Lotus's stereo_crossfade: the equal-power crossfade carried into stereo, one shared law, both channels in lockstep
prompt Continue the recurring baton (prin recur) on the ALES/Lotus edge, Lindy-first crux-first; approve my own design rounds; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates.
think Sixth lap of this recur run. ALES130's fade closed on a named next crux: a stereo fade-out laid under a stereo fade-in IS a stereo crossfade. That is the Lindy-first crux-first pick — it makes an already-recognised gesture its own primitive and continues the stereo amplitude class (normalize ALES129, fade ALES130) with the equal-power crossfade ALES12.
think Read crossfade.rye (ALES12): crossfade(out_clip, in_clip, out) crosses two equal-length overlap Clips with the equal-power weights power.split(i, den) read at each sample, refusing timeline.EditError.BadRange on a length mismatch or an overlap shorter than two samples, saturating loud sums through ALES2. The weights are POSITIONAL (identical at each seat regardless of channel) — the same structure ALES130's ramp had, so the same image-preserving-for-free reasoning applies. Read pan.rye for the StereoClip (two Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant.
think Matched the shared-validation pattern from stereo_fade: assert both masters balanced, check the overlap length once (count == in_master.left.len, count >= 2) against the shared length before either output channel is written, then run mono crossfade per channel with the SAME weights. Because both masters enter balanced at count, one up-front check pre-validates both channel crosses, so a refusal never writes one channel and leaves the other stale. Reused timeline.EditError by name (crossfade adds no new fault).
think Wrote design read, module, witness. Four laws: stereo crossfade law (each channel == ALES12's crossfade of that channel's out/in windows byte for byte, head pure outgoing and tail pure incoming), balance/length law (out.left.len == out.right.len == count), shared-weight image law (the equal-power center lands each channel's own level at the same positional weight — a 1000/2000 field crossed with silence landing 700/1400 at center, and an identical-channel pair staying identical), atomicity/degenerate law (a length mismatch and a too-short overlap each refuse BadRange with the output still empty, a loud overlap saturating on both channels). Witness GREEN; tame_style_check bans clean.
obs The stereo amplitude class now stands as three: the measured-scalar member (normalize, one shared number), the positional-ramp member (fade, one shared ramp), and the positional-weight member (crossfade, one shared equal-power split). All three share the same lesson — a per-channel quantity that is IDENTICAL across channels preserves the stereo image for free, while a quantity MEASURED per channel would split it (the ALES129 linked-normalize red). No custody gate reached — purely local software, self-approved design round.
loom witness=ales_stereo_crossfade_witness result=GREEN laws=4 channels=2 masters=2
file lotus/stereo_crossfade.rye ALES131 — stereo_crossfade, the equal-power crossfade lifted to stereo: two masters crossed over one shared overlap through the proven mono crossfade, validated once, four laws with the shared-weight image crux.
file tools/ales_stereo_crossfade_witness.rish the witness — builds, runs selftest, asserts the GREEN ales-stereo-crossfade line.
file active-designing/20260815-031427_fill-ales131-lotus-stereo-crossfade.md the self-approved design read for this rung.
file lotus/README.md front door synced — ALES131 roster entry on the Status line.
recommend keep-going the stereo amplitude class stands as three (normalize · fade · crossfade); the next mechanical laps lift the remaining value ops (halve, rectify, drive-family) to stereo, each trivial per-channel where the image is unaffected — no gate reached.
